controversy-and-consensus-4 = unlines (cons "The Romans show controversy as dialogue, turns with polarity, directly. A *controversia* was a training exercise in rhetoric: you took a case and argued it from both sides in turn. Seneca the Elder collected them in his *Controversiae*. The practice was turning a question one way and then the other, the same question held from two poles. No one lost. You learned the case by turning it fully." nil)
